Ladybird’s navigator.userAgentData reports the brands, mobileness and
platform of its Sec-CH-UA headers, from the same User-Agent string, and
exists only while the navigator compatibility mode is Chrome.

Blink ships it. NavigatorUA::userAgentData() fills a NavigatorUAData
from the embedder’s UserAgentMetadata, which GetUserAgentMetadata() in
components/embedder_support/user_agent_utils.cc builds with the brand
list Sec-CH-UA sends, and NavigatorUAData::getHighEntropyValues()
resolves in a task posted to TaskType::kPermission. Its high-entropy
values match Ladybird’s: GetCpuArchitecture() answers “arm” or “x86”,
GetCpuBitness() “64” on Apple platforms, GetFormFactorsClientHint()
“Desktop” or “Mobile” from the mobile bit, and GetPlatformVersion() the
macOS major.minor.bugfix version, or the empty string on Linux.

Where Blink’s User-Agent string is overridden without any metadata,
LocalDOMWindow::GetUserAgentMetadata() falls back to empty metadata, so
its brands and platform are empty — as Ladybird’s are for a User-Agent
string that names no Chromium version. Blink’s toJSON() returns a plain
object; Ladybird’s returns the spec’s UALowEntropyJSON dictionary,
which serializes the same.

Blink’s NavigatorUA::userAgentData() builds a new NavigatorUAData from
the current metadata on every access, and both Blink’s and WebKit’s
brands answer with a new frozen array on every read. Ladybird keeps one
NavigatorUAData for as long as the navigator’s User-Agent string stays
the same, and its bindings keep the brands array for as long as
something holds it. And Blink, like Ladybird, reports the CPU under
Rosetta: Its GetCpuArchitecture() answers “arm” for the kTranslatedIntel
that base::mac::GetCPUType() reads from sysctl.proc_translated.

WebKit implements it, in WebCore’s NavigatorUAData.cpp, but leaves it
off by default, behind the NavigatorUserAgentDataJavaScriptAPIEnabled
setting. With a custom User-Agent string, it parses that string into its
brands and withholds the high-entropy values. Gecko doesn’t implement
it. So in the WebKit and Gecko compatibility modes, Ladybird has neither
NavigatorUAData nor userAgentData.
